## What this terminal has that neither reference does

This is the reason to build it at all.

**Backing, as a first-class figure.** Not a footnote. The stat strip carries
backing per token beside price, and a ratio of market price to backing. On a
token with no treasury it reads "not ballasted" plainly rather than showing a
zero.

**A treasury panel.** What the project actually holds, asset by asset: ticker,
quantity, Chainlink price, USD value, and when that feed last published. If a
feed is stale, say so and say how stale — an old price on SGOV is normal, and
the panel should teach that rather than alarm.

**Withdrawal state.** The notice period this project chose, whether a
withdrawal has been announced, and if so a countdown to when it could execute.
This is the single most useful thing a trader can know that no other terminal
shows.

**Creator holding, which is always zero.** Where other terminals show a dev
holding percentage that varies, ours reads 0% on every token, always, with a
line explaining there is no allocation mechanism in the contracts. The constant
is the point.

**A source on every figure.** Each number carries where it came from and how
old it is, using the existing `<Freshness>` component. On-chain reads at 12s,
GeckoTerminal and Blockscout at 30s.

